        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br> <p> Immune system dysregulation is associated with space flight due to the combination of microgravity, stress, circadian misalignment and/or radiation. Alterations in leukocyte distribution, reduced cellular function, or altered cytokine profiles characterizes immune system dysregulation. The dysregulation of adaptive immunity observed during flight has not been studied in ground analogs. This experiment seeks to collect low cost pilot data assessing stress, immunity, and viral reactivation during a winterover in Antarctica at the U.S. Palmer Station. </p><p> Ground analogs are appropriate for fluid shifts or bone and muscle loss but are not representative of the immune system dysregulation. An appropriate flight analog will be able to replicate stress, isolation, confinement of extreme environment, prolonged isolation and periods of 24 hours of darkness. Pilot data from the coastal Neumayer station in Germany indicates that significant immune alterations similar to space flight manifest during coastal winterover at an interior station. Coastal Antarctica winterover may be a more superior location to validate space flight immunology countermeasures due to its Normoxic environment. The goal is to assess if the Palmer Station may serve as a space flight analog option for ground validation of immune countermeasures. </p><p> This experiment has the following specific aims:</p><ol>
<li> Investigate the effect of coastal Antarctica winterover on salivary stress hormones, salivary cytokine profiles, plasma cytokines, and basic leukocyte distribution. </li>
<li> Investigate the effect of coastal Antarctica winterover on the reactivation and shedding of latent herpesviruses. </li>
<li> Characterize other adverse clinical outcomes, as voluntarily shared by crewmembers via a health survey, such that immune changes, viral reactivation, and clinical manifestations may be correlated to infer information regarding clinical risk from persistent immune dysregulation. </li>
 </ol>

APPROACH:<br> <p> Approximately, 17 to 30 subjects will be recruited for the experiment. Subjects will collect a passive drool saliva sample, label the vial, and then freeze the sample for return. The saliva samples will be used to assess stress hormones, cytokines, and latent herpesvirus DNA. The salivary cytokine profile will be assessed by cytometric bead multiplex array. The presence of viral DNA and cortisol concentrations will be assessed through polymerase chain reaction and ELISA, respectively. Subjects will also collect a fingerstick blood sample and analyze the blood using the hand-held blood analyzer. The hand-held fingerstick blood analyzer will produce basic leukocyte subsets that include absolute and differential white blood counts (WBC). Subjects will also be given the option to complete a health survey on the last day of each collection timepoint. A hair sample will be collected from subjects and analyzed for cortisol level. Samples will be returned to Johnson Space Center (JSC) approximately three to six months after the winterover mission is completed. Due to the number of samples, analysis can take up to six months after receipt.</p><p>
RESULTS: <br></p><p>It is anticipated that winterover at a coastal Antarctica station will result in a pattern of immune dysregulation&nbsp;<span style="background-color: white;">similar to that observed in astronauts onboard the International Space Station (ISS). This is a result of normoxic&nbsp;</span><span style="background-color: white;">conditions in an extreme environment, circadian misalignment, isolation, stress, and station lifestyle.</span></p><p></p>
        </div>

